At temperature, $T_{1}=300 \mathrm{~K}$
Average kinetic energy, $E_{1}=6.21 \times 10^{-21} \mathrm{~J}$
\therefore \quad T_{2}=600 \mathrm{~K}, E_{2}=\text { ? }

We know that, average kinetic energy of gas molecule,

\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{E_{2}}{E_{1}} &=\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \Rightarrow E_{2}=\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \times E_{1} \\
&=\frac{600}{300} \times 6.21 \times 10^{-21} \\
&=2 \times 6.21 \times 10^{-21} \\
&=12.42 \times 10^{-21} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}
